<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Girolamo Riario</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1443–1488</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Girolamo Riario was a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a>, who acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imola">Imola</a> for him in 1473 and added Forlì in 1480. He married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caterina-sforza">Caterina Sforza</a>, daughter of the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and was implicated in the Pazzi conspiracy against the Medici in 1478. Conspirators murdered him in Forlì in 1488, and Caterina held the state for their son.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/girolamo-riario.jpg" alt="Girolamo Riario" width="253" height="349" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sandro Botticelli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Girolamo_Riario_(The_Trials_of_Moses,_Botticelli).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
